      "chapterNumber": 7,
      "headline": "Check the Mirror: Confirm Your Comprehension",
      "keyInsight": "Most people treat conversation like a game of catch where nobody looks at the ball. You assume you caught the message, but you actually caught your own interpretation of it. Here is the funny paradox: repeating someone's exact words feels awkward to you, yet it feels deeply magical to them. When you play back their thoughts in your own words, you are not testing their intelligence. You are holding up a clear mirror. Most people speak to discover what they actually think. By summarizing what you heard, you hand them an audio recording of their own mind. It turns out that asking 'Did I get that right?' is not a sign of poor hearing. It is the ultimate social superglue.",
      "content": "Imagine ordering coffee and the barista hands you a cup of warm soup. You asked for a latte, but they heard something else. In daily talk, we hand each other emotional soup all day long. Someone shares a tough day at work, and we reply to an entirely different problem. We nod along while our brains build a fake version of their story. To stop this guessing game, you need a quick feedback loop.\n\nThink of comprehension like adjusting the side mirrors before driving onto a highway. You do not just hit the gas pedal and pray the blind spots disappear. You check the glass. In conversation, checking the mirror sounds like: 'It sounds like you felt completely ignored during that meeting. Is that right?' This tiny pause gives the speaker a chance to calibrate. They can say 'Yes, exactly!' or 'Well, not ignored, more like rushed.' Either way, the fog clears instantly.\n\nTry using three simple kitchen tools: mirroring, paraphrasing, and labeling feelings. Mirroring is simply repeating the last two or three words they said with a curious tone. Paraphrasing is taking their messy recipe and naming the final dish in your own words. Labeling names the quiet emotion bubbling beneath the surface, like noticing steam rising from a kettle. When you name the steam, the pressure drops immediately.\n\nWhen people feel accurately heard, their entire nervous system relaxes. Their shoulders drop, their breathing slows down, and their defenses dissolve. They no longer have to yell or repeat themselves to prove their point. You have handed them proof of reception. The conversation transforms from two people talking over each other into a smooth, shared rhythm.",
